  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem requires finding a step function that minimizes the maximum overlap between the function and its shifted versions. Current approaches using binary representations and local search have reached a bound of 0.380865. This strategy employs a wavelet-like construction with multi-frequency sinusoidal components to create smoother transitions between steps, reducing overlap peaks. By optimizing a continuous representation, we avoid the discontinuity issues in binary step functions and allow for more effective gradient-based optimization.\n\nThe key improvement is using a continuous representation that combines sine and cosine terms with adaptive frequency selection, unlocking better overlap reduction than current integer-based shifts. The mathematically formulated approach ensures global optimality potential while maintaining feasibility constraints.\n\nMathematical specification:\nRepresent the function as:\nh(x) = D(x) + \u2211_{m=1}^{M} (A_m sin(2\u03c0f_m x) + B_m cos(2\u03c0f_m x)\nwhere:\n- D(x) is a damping factor ensuring 0\u2264h\u22641\n- f_m are optimal frequencies\n- A_m, B_m are coefficients to optimize\n- M is the number of frequency components\n\nConstraints:\n1. Integral constraint: \u2211_{i}h_i = n_points/2\n2. Bound constraints: min(h) \u2265 0, max(h) \u2264 1\n\nOverlap objective:\nC5 = max_k \u2211_i h_i (1-h_i+k) dx / n\n\nThe representation ensures no discontinuities while automatically satisfying integral constraint. Frequencies start from smooth components (low frequencies) and extend to higher frequencies for micro-patterns.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization with parent:\n   - Use provided initial_h_values if available\n   - Convert to coefficient representation via FFT\n   - Project to [0,1] using exponential clipping\n\n2. Forward model:\n   - Generate samples from coefficients: h[i] = f(x_i) clipped to [0,1]\n   - Compute integral via \u2211h*dx to adjust overall intensity\n\n3. Optimization loop:\n   - Frequency mutation: perturb active frequencies with Gaussian noise\n   - Gradient-based adjustment: use covariance matrix adaptation (CMA-ES)\n   - Overlap evaluation: fast FFT-based correlation scores\n   - Restart policy: restart with 20% frequency change if improvement stalls\n\n4. Candidate acceptance:\n   - Strictly compare raw overlap scores\n   - Maintain last 5 stable improvements\n   - Score validity check: ensure 0\u2264h\u22641\n\n5. Termination and fallback:\n   - Return best candidate found\n   - Fallback solution: nearest binary version of parent\n   - Timeout: 450 seconds\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Frequency levels (low to high): 5-15 components\n- Amplitude range: [-0.4,0.4] for sine/cosine components\n- Damping factor: exponential decay 0.9-0.999\n- Population size: 20-40 (CMA-ES parameter)\n\nComputebudget:\n- Dominant cost: FFT operations (O(n log n))\n- 600 evaluations: ~60 seconds\n- Fallback enabled for large shifts\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. Verify integral is 0.5 \u00b1 1e-6\n2. Check all values in [0,1]\n3. Re-evaluate final candidate with exact overlap calculation\n4. Return (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) with h_values representing continuous function",
